    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> kingdom on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>, which grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and on gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> sent to the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> walls, up to 11 metres high in the Great Enclosure, are the largest precolonial stone structures in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</li><li>Power passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> and Butua states after about 1450, but the ruins gave the modern nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> its name and emblem.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> Bird on the national flag copies soapstone carvings from the ruins; one was taken by a hunter in 1889 and sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, and four were returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1981.</p></aside>
    </section>

