      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Rise &amp; Fall</h2>
      <figure class="rf-chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 206" role="img" aria-label="Relative power of the Kingdom of Zimbabwe over time, peaking in 1380 CE"><line class="rf-base" x1="20" x2="740" y1="160" y2="160"/><path class="rf-area" d="M20.0 133.2 L113.9 119.8 L239.1 93.0 L333.0 66.2 L427.0 39.4 L520.9 26.0 L614.8 39.4 L677.4 66.2 L708.7 93.0 L740.0 133.2 L740.0 160 L20.0 160 Z"/><path class="rf-line" d="M20.0 133.2 L113.9 119.8 L239.1 93.0 L333.0 66.2 L427.0 39.4 L520.9 26.0 L614.8 39.4 L677.4 66.2 L708.7 93.0 L740.0 133.2"/><circle class="rf-peak" cx="520.9" cy="26.0" r="5"/><text class="rf-peak-label" x="520.9" y="16.0" text-anchor="middle">Peak 1380 CE</text><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="20.0" x2="20.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="20.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="20.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">2</text><title>c. 1200: Builders begin enclosing the Hill Complex with dry-stone walls</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="427.0" x2="427.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="427.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="427.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">3</text><title>14th century: The Great Enclosure and its conical tower are built south of the hill</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">4</text><title>c. 1450: Great Zimbabwe loses its role as the Mutapa and Butua states rise</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1220 CE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">1450 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000</span><span class="rf-text">Farming communities settle the hill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200</span><span class="rf-text">Builders begin enclosing the Hill Complex with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> walls</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Enclosure and its conical tower are built south of the hill</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1450</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> loses its role as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> and Butua states rise</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1531</span><span class="rf-text">Vicente Pegado, captain at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, records the name ‘Symbaoe’ for the stone buildings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text">Karl Mauch visits the ruins and attributes them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">Gertrude Caton-Thompson’s excavations confirm the site’s African and medieval origin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text">Independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> takes its name from the ruins</span></li></ol>
