    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Great Zimbabwe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Zimbabwe grew up on the granite country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> rivers, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> farmers had lived since the first millennium. Its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>, lies near the modern town of Masvingo. Farming communities whose pottery archaeologists call Gumanye, regarded as ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karanga">Karanga</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>, lived at the site from around 1000, and from about the 12th century a ruling group began to enclose its granite hill with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> walls: the radiocarbon dates from the site support building between the 12th and the 15th century. Archaeologists disagree about how the kingdom arose. Thomas Huffman and others see it as the heir of the Kingdom of Mapungubwe on the Limpopo, which was abandoned around 1290 as trade shifted north towards the goldfields of the plateau; Shadreck Chirikure and his colleagues argue that Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> grew up alongside Mapungubwe as one of several competing centres. Either way, control of the routes carrying gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> to the coast, together with wealth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, allowed its rulers to raise the largest precolonial stone buildings in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Its name is usually derived from the Shona <em>dzimba-dze-mabwe</em>, ‘houses of stone’.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sofala.jpg" alt="Sofala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sofala1683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mallet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> were the main store of wealth on the plateau, and the court appears to have controlled large herds that were moved between seasonal pastures. Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, and the capital drew food and tribute from the surrounding countryside. The kingdom’s wealth rested above all on gold, which was mined from shallow workings and recovered from streams across the plateau, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. Both were carried towards the coast along routes following the Save and Runde rivers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, where merchants from Kilwa shipped them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. In return the rulers received glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, cloth, Chinese celadon and later blue-and-white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, Persian glazed pottery and glass from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coin minted at Kilwa has been found at the site. Crucibles and working debris show that gold was also worked in the city into beads and wire. Estimates of the capital’s population at its height range from under 10,000 to about 18,000. Most people lived in round houses of <em>daga</em>, a mixture of granite sand and clay, while the elite lived within stone enclosures. Soapstone carvings, among them the famous birds, iron gongs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-ingot">copper ingots</a> and finely made pottery show the work of skilled craftsmen serving the court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Very little is known of warfare in the Kingdom of Zimbabwe, which left no written records, and archaeologists still debate how far its rulers relied on force. The great walls of the capital were not built mainly as fortifications: their chief purpose seems to have been to display royal prestige and to screen the ruler and the elite from public view rather than to withstand attack. Yet the Hill Complex, a rocky granite height whose enclosures are reached by narrow, partly covered passages, was easy to defend, and some scholars think that the outer walls of the city also had a defensive role. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> spearheads and ritual spears are among the finds at the capital. The kingdom’s reach over some 150 lesser stone-walled centres implies that its rulers could command tribute and labour, perhaps backed by the threat of force as well as by ritual authority and gifts of imported goods. Whether provincial chiefs ever rebelled, or whether the capital fought its neighbours, is unknown. Portuguese writers of the 16th century described the armies of its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state, but how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>’s own kings made war cannot now be reconstructed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No ruler of the Kingdom of Zimbabwe is known by name from contemporary evidence, and its government must be reconstructed from archaeology, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> practice and Portuguese reports. The king, known in later Shona states as the <em>mambo</em>, appears to have combined political and religious authority, linking his people with the royal ancestors and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwari-cult">Mwari</a>, the high god. The Hill Complex, which overlooks the city, held enclosures that were probably royal residences and places of ritual; in its Eastern Enclosure were found monoliths topped by carved soapstone birds, perhaps royal or sacred emblems. Archaeologists disagree about how the different parts of the capital were used. One view holds that the Hill Complex, the Great Enclosure and the Valley Ruins were the residences of successive kings, built as the centre of power moved; another, argued above all by Thomas Huffman, reads them as parts of a single capital with separate royal, ritual and domestic spaces. Beyond the capital, smaller walled sites in the same style across the plateau were probably the seats of provincial chiefs who owed tribute to the king, and the state may have covered some 50,000 square kilometres. The stone walls themselves expressed the social order, separating the ruling class from the commoners who lived outside them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khami"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khami.jpg" alt="Khami" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZW_Khami_Ruins.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Digr, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> gave its name to a whole tradition of building and rule. Hundreds of stone-walled sites in the same style, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, the capital of the Kingdom of Butua, and Manyikeni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, continued the forms developed there, and traditions trace the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state to its royal family. The gold sent through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> underpinned the wealth of Kilwa and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns. Portuguese writers of the 16th century, among them Vicente Pegado, captain at Sofala, in 1531 and the historian João de Barros, described the walls from traders’ reports. After the hunter Adam Render showed the ruins to the German explorer Karl Mauch in 1871, colonial writers attributed them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and James Theodore Bent, digging with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a> in 1891, favoured foreign builders. David Randall-MacIver in 1905 and Gertrude Caton-Thompson in 1929 showed that the site was African and medieval, but the Rhodesian government later pressed for non-African origins to be presented, and the archaeologist Peter Garlake was forced to leave the country in 1970. On independence in 1980 the new state took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and the soapstone bird as its emblem, and the ruins became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1986.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zambezi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zambezi.jpg" alt="Zambezi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zambezi_River_at_junction_of_Namibia,_Zambia,_Zimbabwe_%26_Botswana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brian McMorrow, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>’s power waned in the 15th century, and by about 1450 it had ceased to be the political centre of the plateau. The causes are debated. A large population with its herds may have exhausted the surrounding grazing, farmland and firewood; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> tradition speaks of a shortage of salt, which may be a way of describing such depletion. Trade also shifted. New routes along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> valley, opened by traders from Angoche on the coast, reached goldfields north and west of Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and international demand for gold appears to have slackened in the early 15th century. Offshoots of the ruling elite founded new states. Shona traditions tell of a prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyatsimba-mutota">Nyatsimba Mutota</a>, who led his followers north in search of salt and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> state, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torwa-dynasty">Torwa dynasty</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a> in the south-west the capital of the Kingdom of Butua. When the Portuguese reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> in 1505, these two states divided the plateau between them. Great Zimbabwe itself was not deserted at once: radiocarbon dates show occupation into the 16th and probably the 17th century, and João de Barros reported that it was kept by an officer of the Mutapa king, with some of the king’s wives living there.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Zimbabwe, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> between the 13th and 15th centuries, controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> of the southern African plateau and raised the largest precolonial stone buildings in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, whose African origin colonial writers long denied and which gave the modern nation its name.</em></p>

